  "question_text": "<p>What buck converter specification dictates the required size of the external inductor and capacitors?</p>",
  "answer_text": "<p>Switching Frequency (f_sw).</p>",
  "explanation_text": "<p>Higher switching frequencies allow you to use physically smaller inductors and capacitors, but at the cost of higher switching losses and lower efficiency.</p>\n<img src=\"{{image_1}}\" alt=\"Schematic of a synchronous buck converter highlighting the switching node (SW), inductor (L), and output capacitor (Cout), overlayed with a graph of inductor current ripple.\" style=\"max-width:100%;margin:1rem 0;border-radius:6px;\"/>",
  "bonus_question_text": "<p>Why choose a synchronous buck converter over an asynchronous one?</p>",
  "bonus_answer_text": "<p>It replaces the diode with a low-side MOSFET, reducing conduction losses and improving efficiency.</p>",
